{
   "id": "ITM-GEN-08011-16",
   "archetype_id": "BC-QA-08011",
   "variant_id": "BC-QV-08011-01",
   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( f(x) = 2 e^{- \\frac{x^{2}}{3}} \\) and \\( g(x) = - \\frac{3 x}{2} \\). The region R shown is bounded by the graphs of f and g and the vertical lines \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = 2 \\). R is the base of a solid whose cross sections perpendicular to the x-axis are equilateral triangles. Using a calculator, find the volume of the solid. Show the setup for the calculation, and give the value correct to three decimal places.",
      "setup_required": true,
      "command_verb": "find"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "numeric",
      "mathjson": 7.30856204545205,
      "decimals": 3
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The side of each cross section is the vertical distance across R, \\( s = f(x) - g(x) = \\frac{3 x}{2} + 2 e^{- \\frac{x^{2}}{3}} \\).",
         "rule_named": "distance between the boundary curves"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Because an equilateral triangle with side s has area (sqrt(3)/4) s^2, each cross section has area \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}s^{2} \\).",
         "rule_named": "area of the named shape"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The volume is \\( \\int_{0}^{2} \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\left(\\frac{3 x}{2} + 2 e^{- \\frac{x^{2}}{3}}\\right)^{2}\\,dx \\), which a calculator gives as about 7.309.",
         "mathjson": 7.30856204545205,
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99001",
         "rule_named": "volume by cross sections"
      }
   ],
   "options": [
